  "name": "Survey of Subsistence Fish and Shellfish for Exposure to Oil Spilled from the Exxon Valdez: First Year: 1989",
  "description": "This report references oil contaminant data of invertebrate communities and invertebrate species: Littorina spp., Mytilus trossulus, Protothaca staminea, and Saxidomus giganteus from Cook Inlet, Kodiak Island, and Prince William Sound, Alaska. Referenced areas include: Akhiok, Chenega Bay, Chiniak, Karluk, Kasitsna Bay, Kodiak, Larsen Bay, Old Harbor, Ouzinkie, Port Graham, Port Lions, Tatitlek, and Windy Bay, Alaska. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Varanasi, U. et. al. 1990. Survey of subsistence fish and shellfish for exposure to oil spilled from the Exxon Valdez: first year: 1989. NOAA Technical Memorandum NMFS F/NWC-191, Seattle, Washington. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #1030 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
